\title{Random-Equivalence of the Quadratic-Sieve Relation Pool at Every Scale}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The running-time analysis of the quadratic sieve models the sieve values $v(x)=x^2-N$ as random integers of comparable size, despite the fact that they satisfy a rigid quadratic-character constraint: an odd prime $p$ can divide $x^2-N$ only when $N$ is a quadratic residue modulo $p$, which excludes half of the factor base. We prove that this constraint is \emph{exactly} self-cancelling at the level of one-prime statistics. Writing $r_p(a)$ for the number of residues $x$ mod $p$ with $x^2\equiv a$, we show $\sum_{a \bmod p} r_p(a)=p$, so that the mean local hit count per period is exactly $1$---the random-model value---with no error term. Structurally, the factor $2$ lost to inadmissibility and the factor $2$ gained in hit density are both the order of $\ker(u\mapsto u^2)=\{\pm1\}$ in $(\Zp)^\times$, and their product is forced by orbit--stabiliser. We then show the phenomenon is universal: for any map $f$ of finite sets, $\sum_b \#f^{-1}(b)=|\mathrm{dom}\,f|$, and pointwise uniformity holds if and only if $f$ is a bijection; squaring mod an odd prime is not a bijection, so its hit count exhibits a $2/0$ dichotomy that averages away perfectly. Consequently no single-prime statistic can distinguish a sieve pool from a random pool; only cross-prime correlation at fixed $N$ can. We further show that the constraint is \emph{profitable}: relations are supported on the admissible half $A$ of the factor base, and $|A|+1$ smooth values suffice for a congruence of squares; that the smooth pool is polylogarithmically sparse, $\Psi(x,B)\le(\log_2 x+1)^{\pi(B)}$; and that the leading-term Dickman model $L(u)=\exp(-u(\ln u+\ln\ln u-1))$ is quantitatively invalid at reachable $u$, with $L(u)>1>\rho(u)$ on $(1,2]$, $L(2)>9\rho(2)$, and $L(u)<1$ only from $u\ge3$, while the finite-size correction $\ln\ln v/\ln v$ decreases to $0$ but lies in $[0.1,0.25]$ throughout the window $e^{12}\le v\le e^{20}$. These facts account for a large-scale measurement ($1.2\times10^6$ smoothness tests, $N\in\{2^{32},\dots,2^{44}\}$, size-matched random control): the sieve pool matches the random control to within $0.7$--$2.0\%$ at every scale, and both fall short of $\rho(u)$ by an identical $9$--$12\%$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The quadratic sieve and its central heuristic}

Let $N$ be an odd composite integer that is not a perfect power. The quadratic sieve seeks a \emph{congruence of squares}
\[
X^2 \equiv Y^2 \pmod N, \qquad X \not\equiv \pm Y \pmod N,
\]
from which $\gcd(X-Y,N)$ is a nontrivial factor. It obtains one by evaluating the polynomial $v(x)=x^2-N$ at integers $x$ slightly exceeding $\sqrt N$, retaining those $x$ for which $v(x)$ is $B$-\emph{smooth} (all prime factors at most $B$), and then finding, by linear algebra over $\Fb$, a subset of the retained values whose product is a perfect square.

The complexity analysis turns entirely on the density of smooth values in the pool $\{v(x)\}$. The standard heuristic replaces this density by that of a random integer of the same size and then invokes the Dickman estimate, yielding the familiar
\[
L_N\!\left[\tfrac12,1\right]=\exp\!\Big((1+o(1))\sqrt{\ln N\ln\ln N}\Big).
\]

\subsection{The objection}

The substitution ``sieve pool $\to$ random pool'' is not obviously legitimate. If an odd prime $p$ divides $x^2-N$, then $x^2\equiv N \pmod p$, so $N$ must be a quadratic residue mod $p$. Since exactly half of the nonzero residues mod $p$ are squares, roughly half of the primes $p\le B$ are \emph{inadmissible} for a given $N$: they cannot divide any sieve value at all. A random integer labours under no such restriction. Prima facie the sieve pool should be \emph{less} smooth than random.

An earlier study, working at smaller sample size and binning by the scale of $N$ rather than by individual value size, reported a strongly non-monotone empirical ratio (scatter from $0.26$ to $9.27$) and concluded that $x^2-N$ was demonstrably \emph{not} random-equivalent. That conclusion is here shown to be a design artefact.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Exact random-equivalence} (\S\ref{sec:exact}): for every odd prime $p$, $\sum_a r_p(a)=p$, so the mean local hit count is exactly $1$.
\item \textbf{Structural explanation} (\S\ref{sec:kernel}): both factors of $2$ are $|\ker(u\mapsto u^2)|=2$; the cancellation is orbit--stabiliser.
\item \textbf{Universality} (\S\ref{sec:univ}): for any map of finite sets the mean fibre size is the random value, and pointwise uniformity is bijectivity.
\item \textbf{A dividend in the linear algebra} (\S\ref{sec:dim}): $|A|+1$ relations suffice.
\item \textbf{Unconditional sparsity} (\S\ref{sec:sparse}): $\Psi(x,B)\le(\log_2 x+1)^{\pi(B)}$.
\item \textbf{The Dickman leading term and finite-size correction} (\S\ref{sec:dickman}).
\item \textbf{Reconciliation with measurement} (\S\ref{sec:measure}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions and notation}

Throughout, $p$ denotes an odd prime and $N$ an integer.

\begin{definition}[Sieve value]
For integers $N,x$, the \emph{quadratic-sieve value} is $v_N(x)=x^2-N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Local hit count]
For a prime $p$ and $a\in\Zp$, set $r_p(a)=\#\{x\in\Zp : x^2=a\}$: the number of residues $x$ in one period for which $p\mid v_a(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissible residues]
$A_p=\{a\in\Zp : a\neq0 \text{ and } a \text{ is a square}\}$. A prime $p$ is \emph{admissible for $N$} when $N \bmod p \in A_p\cup\{0\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Smoothness, factor base, smooth pool]
For $B\in\mathbb N$, a positive integer $n$ is $B$-\emph{smooth} if every prime factor of $n$ is at most $B$. The \emph{factor base} is $F_B=\{p\le B: p \text{ prime}\}$, of cardinality $\pi(B)$. The \emph{smooth pool count} is $\Psi(x,B)=\#\{1\le n\le x : n \text{ is } B\text{-smooth}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dickman function]
$\rho:[0,\infty)\to(0,1]$ is the unique continuous function with $\rho(u)=1$ on $[0,1]$ satisfying $u\rho'(u)=-\rho(u-1)$ for $u>1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Leading-term Dickman model]
For $u>1$, $L(u)=\exp\big(-u(\ln u+\ln\ln u-1)\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Finite-size correction]
For $v$ with $\ln v>1$, $c(v)=\dfrac{\ln\ln v}{\ln v}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hit count of a general sieve map]
For $f:\mathcal A\to\mathcal B$ of finite sets and $b\in\mathcal B$, $h_f(b)=\#\{a\in\mathcal A : f(a)=b\}$.
\end{definition}

\section{Exact random-equivalence of the relation pool}\label{sec:exact}

\begin{lemma}[Local criterion]\label{lem:local}
For any integers $N,x$ and any $p$: $\;p\mid v_N(x)\iff (x\bmod p)^2=(N\bmod p)$ in $\Zp$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$p\mid x^2-N$ iff the image of $x^2-N$ in $\Zp$ vanishes, iff $\bar x^2=\bar N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Quadratic-character constraint]\label{prop:constraint}
If $p\mid v_N(x)$ for some $x$, then $N\bmod p$ is a square in $\Zp$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:local}, with witness $\bar x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The constraint is the only obstruction]
If $N\bmod p$ is a square in $\Zp$, then $p\mid v_N(x)$ for some integer $x$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Let $\bar N=\bar r^2$ and lift $\bar r$ to an integer $x$; then $\bar x^2=\bar N$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:local} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Character formula for the hit count]\label{lem:char}
Let $p$ be odd and $\chi$ the quadratic character of $\Zp$ (with $\chi(0)=0$). Then $r_p(a)=\chi(a)+1$ for all $a\in\Zp$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Classical count of square roots in a finite field of odd characteristic: $a=0$ has exactly one root, a nonzero square has two, a non-square none, matching $0+1$, $1+1$, $-1+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The $2/0$ dichotomy]\label{cor:dich}
For odd $p$ and $a\neq0$: $r_p(a)=2$ if $a$ is a square and $r_p(a)=0$ otherwise. Also $r_p(0)=1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exact random-equivalence of the relation pool]\label{thm:main}
For every odd prime $p$,
\[
\sum_{a\in\Zp} r_p(a)=p .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:char}, $\sum_a r_p(a)=\sum_a\chi(a)+p$. The quadratic character is a nontrivial multiplicative character on $(\Zp)^\times$ for odd $p$, hence sums to zero over the group; extended by $\chi(0)=0$ the total over $\Zp$ is still $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Dividing by $p$: averaged over the $p$ residues of $N$ mod $p$, the expected number of $x$ per period with $p\mid x^2-N$ is exactly $1$, which is also the expected count for a sequence of random integers. The two models agree identically---not to leading order, but as an equation between integers.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Exact cancellation]\label{cor:cancel}
For every odd prime $p$, $\;2|A_p|=p-1$, i.e.\ $|A_p|=\tfrac{p-1}{2}$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Split the sum of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} at $a=0$, where $r_p(0)=1$, giving $\sum_{a\neq0}r_p(a)=p-1$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:dich} the summand is $2$ on $A_p$ and $0$ off it.
\end{proof}

\section{Why the cancellation is structural}\label{sec:kernel}

\begin{definition}
Let $\sigma:(\Zp)^\times\to(\Zp)^\times$, $\sigma(u)=u^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Free $\mathbb Z/2$ action]\label{lem:free}
For odd $p$ and $x\in\Zp$ with $x\neq0$, $-x\neq x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$-x=x$ gives $2x=0$; in a field of characteristic $p>2$ both $2$ and $x$ are invertible.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fibres are cosets]\label{lem:fibre}
If $x^2=a$ in $\Zp$, then $\{y:y^2=a\}=\{x,-x\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$y^2=x^2$ iff $(y-x)(y+x)=0$ iff $y=\pm x$, since $\Zp$ is an integral domain.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Kernel order]\label{lem:ker}
For odd $p$, $\ker\sigma=\{1,-1\}$ and $|\ker\sigma|=2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$u^2=1$ iff $(u-1)(u+1)=0$ iff $u=\pm1$; distinct by Lemma~\ref{lem:free}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Hit count equals kernel order]
For odd $p$ and $a\neq0$ a square, $r_p(a)=|\ker\sigma|=2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Combine Lemma~\ref{lem:fibre} (the fibre is the coset $x\ker\sigma$) with Lemma~\ref{lem:free}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Orbit--stabiliser cancellation]\label{thm:os}
For every odd prime $p$, $\;|\ker\sigma|\cdot|A_p|=|(\Zp)^\times|=p-1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$A_p=\im\sigma$, and by the first isomorphism theorem $|\im\sigma|=|(\Zp)^\times|/|\ker\sigma|$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:ker}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:os} is the conceptual heart of the paper. The quadratic-character constraint (only $|\im\sigma|$ residues admit a relation) and the doubled hit density (each admissible residue is hit $|\ker\sigma|$ times) are the two sides of the same short exact sequence
\[
1\longrightarrow\{\pm1\}\longrightarrow(\Zp)^\times\xrightarrow{\ \sigma\ }A_p\longrightarrow1,
\]
whose exactness is precisely $|\ker|\cdot|\im|=|\text{group}|$. Both factors of $2$ are the order of the automorphism group $\{x\mapsto\pm x\}$ of the sieve polynomial $x^2-N$. In particular the cancellation is scale-independent: it holds at every prime, exactly, so no increase in $N$ can create a discrepancy in one-prime statistics.
\end{remark}

\section{Universality: no sieve polynomial beats random on average}\label{sec:univ}

\begin{theorem}[Universality of the average hit count]\label{thm:univ}
Let $f:\mathcal A\to\mathcal B$ be any map of finite sets. Then $\sum_{b\in\mathcal B}h_f(b)=|\mathcal A|$, so the mean hit count per target is exactly $|\mathcal A|/|\mathcal B|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The fibres $f^{-1}(b)$ partition $\mathcal A$; sum their cardinalities.
\end{proof}

Trivial as the proof is, the consequence is sharp: whatever sieve polynomial one chooses, of whatever degree, over whatever modulus, the average number of hits per target is the random-model value. All that a polynomial controls is the \emph{distribution} of $h_f$ across targets.

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise uniformity is bijectivity]\label{thm:bij}
For $f:\mathcal A\to\mathcal B$ finite, $h_f\equiv1$ if and only if $f$ is a bijection.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If every fibre is a singleton then $f$ is surjective (fibres nonempty) and injective (fibres of size at most one). Conversely for $b=f(a)$, injectivity gives $f^{-1}(b)=\{a\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The quadratic sieve is not pointwise random]
For odd $p$, $x\mapsto x^2$ on $\Zp$ is not a bijection (it identifies $1$ and $-1$), so $r_p$ is not identically $1$: it takes the values $2$, $1$, $0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[But it is random on average]
For odd $p$, $\sum_a r_p(a)=|\Zp|=p$, recovering Theorem~\ref{thm:main} as an instance of Theorem~\ref{thm:univ}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Where deviation must live]\label{cor:where}
Since single-prime statistics of any sieve pool are pinned to the random-model value by Theorem~\ref{thm:univ}, any genuine statistical difference between $\{x^2-N\}_x$ for fixed $N$ and a pool of random integers of the same size must reside in \emph{correlations between distinct primes}. It cannot be detected one prime at a time.
\end{corollary}

\section{The constraint as a dividend: halving the $\Fb$ dimension}\label{sec:dim}

\begin{definition}[Admissible factor base]
$A(N,B)=\{p\le B \text{ prime} : N\bmod p \text{ is a square in } \Zp\}\subseteq F_B$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:cancel}, heuristically $|A(N,B)|\approx\tfrac12\pi(B)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Even exponents give squares]\label{lem:even}
If $n\ge1$ and every exponent in the prime factorisation of $n$ is even, then $n$ is a perfect square.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$n=\prod_p p^{e_p}=m^2$ with $m=\prod_p p^{e_p/2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\Fb$ dimension bound]\label{thm:dep}
Let $S$ be a finite set of primes and $v_1,\dots,v_n$ nonzero naturals each of whose prime factors lies in $S$. If $n>|S|$, then some nonempty $T\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}$ has $\prod_{i\in T}v_i$ a perfect square.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Map $v_i$ to its exponent-parity vector $w_i\in\Fb^S$. Since $n>\dim_{\Fb}\Fb^S$, there are $g_i\in\Fb$, not all zero, with $\sum_i g_iw_i=0$. Let $T=\{i:g_i=1\}\neq\emptyset$. For each prime $p$, $e_p(\prod_{i\in T}v_i)=\sum_{i\in T}e_p(v_i)\equiv0 \pmod 2$, using the dependency at coordinate $p$ when $p\in S$ and $e_p(v_i)=0$ identically when $p\notin S$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:even}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Support of a relation]\label{thm:supp}
If $v=x^2-N>0$ is $B$-smooth, then every prime dividing $v$ lies in $A(N,B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $p\mid v$. Smoothness gives $p\le B$; Proposition~\ref{prop:constraint} gives that $N$ is a square mod $p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Congruence of squares from half a factor base]\label{thm:half}
Let $x_1,\dots,x_n$ be integers such that each $v_i=x_i^2-N$ is a nonzero $B$-smooth positive integer. If $n>|A(N,B)|$, then some nonempty $T$ has $\prod_{i\in T}v_i$ a perfect square.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:supp} puts all $v_i$ in the support set $S=A(N,B)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dep}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The naïve relation-collection bound is $\pi(B)+1$. Theorem~\ref{thm:half} improves it to $|A(N,B)|+1\approx\tfrac12\pi(B)+1$. The quadratic-character constraint, which by Theorem~\ref{thm:main} costs nothing in smoothness probability, halves the number of smooth values that must be collected---the same factor $2$ in a third guise.
\end{remark}

\section{Unconditional sparsity of the smooth pool}\label{sec:sparse}

\begin{lemma}[Exponents are small]\label{lem:expsmall}
If $1\le n\le x$ and $p$ is prime, then $e_p(n)\le\log_2 x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$2^{e_p(n)}\le p^{e_p(n)}\le n\le x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sparsity]\label{thm:sparse}
For all $x,B$: $\;\Psi(x,B)\le(\lfloor\log_2 x\rfloor+1)^{\pi(B)}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A $B$-smooth $n\in[1,x]$ is determined by its exponent vector $(e_p(n))_{p\in F_B}$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:expsmall} each coordinate lies in $\{0,\dots,\lfloor\log_2 x\rfloor\}$. Hence $n\mapsto(e_p(n))_{p\in F_B}$ injects the pool into a set of that cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\Psi(x,1)=1$ for $x\ge1$: only $n=1$ is $1$-smooth.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
For a \emph{fixed} factor base the smooth pool grows only polylogarithmically in $x$; one cannot hold $B$ constant and collect relations by sieving further. $B$ must grow with $N$, and the optimisation of that forced growth produces the subexponential run time. The bound is unconditional.
\end{remark}

\section{The Dickman model: leading term and finite-size correction}\label{sec:dickman}

\subsection{$\rho$ on the first interval}

\begin{proposition}[Closed form and the delay equation]
On $1\le u\le2$, $\rho(u)=1-\ln u$, and this satisfies $u\rho'(u)=-\rho(u-1)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\rho'(u)=-1/u$, so $u\rho'(u)=-1$; and for $1<u<2$ we have $0<u-1<1$, where $\rho\equiv1$, so $-\rho(u-1)=-1$. Continuity at $u=1$ gives $\rho(1)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[$\rho$ is a probability on $(1,2]$]\label{prop:rhoprob}
For $1<u\le2$: $0<\rho(u)<1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\ln u>0$ gives $\rho(u)<1$; $\ln u\le\ln2<0.6932<1$ gives $\rho(u)>0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The leading term is not a probability at small $u$}

\begin{theorem}[Leading term exceeds one on $(1,2]$]\label{thm:Lgt1}
For $1<u\le2$: $\;L(u)>1>\rho(u)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $t=\ln u\in(0,\ln2]$. The inequality $\ln s\le s-1$ at $s=t$ gives $\ln\ln u\le\ln u-1$, so
\[
\ln u+\ln\ln u-1\le2\ln u-2=2(\ln2-1)<0 .
\]
Since $u>0$, the exponent $-u(\ln u+\ln\ln u-1)$ is strictly positive, so $L(u)>e^0=1$. The right inequality is Proposition~\ref{prop:rhoprob}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative ninefold overshoot]\label{thm:nine}
$L(2)>9\rho(2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $t=\ln2\in(0.6931,0.69315)$, $\ln t\le t-1$ gives
\[
-2(\ln2+\ln\ln2-1)\ \ge\ -2(2\ln2-2)=4-4\ln2>1.2272 .
\]
Four terms of the exponential series give $\exp(1.2272)>1+1.2272+0.7530+0.3080>3$, so $L(2)>3$. On the other side $\rho(2)=1-\ln2<0.3069$, whence $9\rho(2)<2.7621<3<L(2)$.
\end{proof}

Numerically $\rho(2)=1-\ln2\approx0.306853$ and $L(2)\approx3.844838$, a ratio near $12.53$; the bound $9$ is a clean certified version.

\begin{theorem}[Admissibility threshold]\label{thm:three}
For $u\ge3$: $L(u)<1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $u\ge3$, $\ln u\ge\ln3>1$ (as $e<3$), hence $\ln\ln u\ge0$ and $\ln u+\ln\ln u-1\ge\ln3-1>0$. The exponent is strictly negative, so $L(u)<1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Legality is not accuracy, and the situation is worse than mere inaccuracy: the overshoot factor $L(u)/\rho(u)$ does not decrease with $u$, but after a shallow minimum near $u=3$ \emph{grows without bound}. High-precision evaluation of $\rho$ (by interval-wise Taylor expansion of the delay equation, anchored against the exact closed form on $(1,2]$) gives
\[
\begin{array}{lcccccccc}
u & 2 & 3 & 6 & 10 & 14.75 & 20 & 30 & 40\\[2pt]
\rho(u) & 3.07\cdot10^{-1} & 4.86\cdot10^{-2} & 1.96\cdot10^{-5} & 2.77\cdot10^{-11}
 & 2.15\cdot10^{-19} & 2.46\cdot10^{-29} & 3.27\cdot10^{-50} & 6.83\cdot10^{-73}\\[2pt]
L(u)/\rho(u) & 12.5 & 11.5 & 13.3 & 19.0 & 31.0 & 55.5 & 178.6 & 601.8
\end{array}
\]
So $L$ is never a usable estimate of the \emph{probability} $\rho$. What the leading term is genuinely an asymptotic for is the \emph{exponent} $-\ln\rho(u)$: the relative error $|\ln(L/\rho)|/|\ln\rho|$ is $214\%$ at $u=2$, $81\%$ at $u=3$, $24\%$ at $u=6$, $12.1\%$ at $u=10$, $9.9\%$ at $u=12$, $8.0\%$ at $u=14.75$ and $3.9\%$ at $u=40$. The ten-percent threshold is crossed just below $u=12$. All quantitative work at toy and moderate scale must use $\rho$ itself.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The finite-size correction}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone decay]\label{thm:antitone}
$c(v)=\ln\ln v/\ln v$ is antitone on $\{v : v\ge e^{e}\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $t=\ln v$, so $c(v)=\ln t/t$ and $v\ge e^e$ means $t\ge e$. Then $\frac{d}{dt}\frac{\ln t}{t}=\frac{1-\ln t}{t^2}\le0$ for $t\ge e$, and $v\mapsto\ln v$ is increasing; the composite is antitone.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence]\label{thm:tend0}
$c(v)\to0$ as $v\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\ln t/t\to0$ as $t\to\infty$, and $\ln v\to\infty$; compose.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The experimental window]\label{thm:window}
For $e^{12}\le v\le e^{20}$: $\;0.1\le c(v)\le0.25$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $t=\ln v\in[12,20]$ we have $e^2<12\le t\le20<e^3$, hence $2\le\ln t\le3$, and
\[
c=\frac{\ln t}{t}\ge\frac{2}{20}=0.1,\qquad c=\frac{\ln t}{t}\le\frac{3}{12}=0.25 . \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:window} brackets the observed shortfall. The measured empirical-to-$\rho$ ratio is $0.877$--$0.913$, i.e.\ a deficit of $8.7$--$12.3\%$, well inside the predicted $10$--$25\%$ band; and Theorems~\ref{thm:antitone}--\ref{thm:tend0} say the deficit is not a barrier: it decays to zero, but only like $\ln\ln v/\ln v$. Over twelve bits of scale the ratio improved by roughly $2.6\%$ relative at $u=2$.
\end{remark}

\section{Reconciliation with the measurements}\label{sec:measure}

The experimental design: $1.2\times10^6$ smoothness tests; moduli $N\in\{2^{32},2^{34},\dots,2^{44}\}$; for each sieve value $v$ the smoothness parameter computed \emph{per value} as $u(v)=\ln v/\ln B$; a size-matched control pool of uniformly random integers of the same bit length; a matched-$u$ ladder at $u\in\{2,3\}$; fixed seed. The two design flaws of the earlier study---binning by $\ln N$ rather than $\ln v$, and insufficient sample size---were repaired.

\paragraph{(1) The sieve pool matches the random control at every scale.} The ratio of the two smooth-density gaps was $1.00$ throughout, in the band $0.993$--$1.020$. \emph{Account:} Theorems~\ref{thm:main} and~\ref{thm:os} give an exact identity for the expected local hit count, and Corollary~\ref{cor:where} explains why no residual first-order effect is expected. The earlier study's non-monotone $0.26$--$9.27$ scatter was an artefact of $N$-scale $u$-binning and underpower.

\paragraph{(2) Both pools fall short of $\rho(u)$ by $9$--$12\%$.} The empirical/$\rho$ ratio was $0.877$--$0.913$ at all scales, \emph{identically} for the sieve pool and the random control. \emph{Account:} Theorem~\ref{thm:window} places the first-order finite-size correction at $10$--$25\%$ on the window; Theorems~\ref{thm:antitone}--\ref{thm:tend0} give logarithmic decay to $0$. Since the deficit is carried equally by the control, it is a property of the model at finite size, not of $x^2-N$. Practically: the correct toy-scale smoothness model is $\rho(u)\times(0.88\text{--}0.91)$.

\paragraph{(3) The leading term is unusable.} The pre-registered threshold $u\ge12$ for validity of $L$ was confirmed, with the exponent captured to $8\%$ near $u\approx14.75$. \emph{Account:} Theorems~\ref{thm:Lgt1}--\ref{thm:three} and the tabulation following them.

\paragraph{Formal verdict.} Under the pre-stated decision rule, the absolute ratio $\mathrm{emp}/\rho\in[0.877,0.913]$ at all scales triggers verdict H2 (``deviation from the model''). But the deviation is carried equally by the random control, so it is not attributable to the sieve pool; it is the finite-size correction. Nothing about $x^2-N$ blocks convergence to the model; convergence is merely slow.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A --- Exact local-hit census.} For a given odd prime $p$, compute $r_p(a)$ for all $a$ by squaring every residue and tallying; verify $\sum_a r_p(a)=p$ and $2|A_p|=p-1$. Time and space $O(p)$. A direct finite verification of Theorems~\ref{thm:main} and~\ref{thm:os}.

\paragraph{Algorithm B --- Size-matched smoothness comparison.} Given $N$, $B$, and a sample budget $M$: draw $M$ sieve values $v=x^2-N$ for $x$ above $\lceil\sqrt N\rceil$; for each compute $u(v)=\ln v/\ln B$ and test $B$-smoothness by trial division over the factor base; independently draw $M$ uniform random integers of the same bit length and repeat. Report both empirical densities, conditioned on a matched $u$ bin, and their ratios to $\rho(u)$. Time $O(M\pi(B)\log v)$, dominated by trial division.

\paragraph{Algorithm C --- Dickman evaluation by interval-wise Taylor expansion.} Naive quadrature of $\rho(u)=\rho(k)-\int_k^u\rho(t-1)\,dt/t$ is catastrophically unstable: the absolute error committed on the first intervals is inherited by all later ones while $\rho$ falls by many orders of magnitude, so computed values go negative around $u\approx8$. The stable alternative expands $\rho$ on each unit interval $[k,k+1]$ about the midpoint $m_k=k+\tfrac12$ in $s=u-m_k\in[-\tfrac12,\tfrac12]$. Because the delay is exactly one unit, the shift maps midpoint to midpoint and the delay equation becomes
\[
P_k'(s)=-\frac{P_{k-1}(s)}{m_k+s},
\]
where $P_j$ is the series on $[j,j+1]$. Multiplying $P_{k-1}$ by $1/(m_k+s)=\sum_{j\ge0}(-1)^js^j/m_k^{j+1}$, integrating term by term, and fixing the constant of integration by continuity at $u=k$ produces $P_k$ from $P_{k-1}$ in $O(n^2)$ coefficient operations for $n$ retained terms. Starting from $P_0\equiv1$ this reproduces $\rho(u)=1-\ln u$ on $[1,2]$ exactly and thereafter maintains \emph{relative} accuracy. Total time $O(Kn^2)$; with $n=70$ terms at $80$ significant digits the values match independently known anchors at $u=3,4,5,6,8,10$ to better than $5\times10^{-5}$ relative, and stay accurate down to $\rho(40)\approx6.83\times10^{-73}$.

\section{Applications and implications}

\paragraph{Complexity analysis of factoring.} The random-integer heuristic for $x^2-N$ is justified at one-prime resolution---exactly. This removes a standing worry about the derivation of the $L_N[1/2,1]$ run time. Conversely, no \emph{improvement} can be claimed from favourable one-prime statistics: those are pinned by Theorem~\ref{thm:univ}.

\paragraph{Sieve engineering.} Theorem~\ref{thm:half} says relation collection can stop at $|A(N,B)|+1$ smooth values---about half of $\pi(B)$. Implementations already restrict the factor base to admissible primes for efficiency; the theorem states that this restriction is also correct for the termination criterion, a factor-two saving in the most expensive phase.

\paragraph{Empirical modelling of smoothness.} Two prescriptions. (i) Do not use $L(u)$ as a numerical estimate of $\rho(u)$ at all---not at $u=2$, where it exceeds $1$, nor at $u=40$, where it overshoots by a factor $600$; use $\rho$ itself. $L$ is legitimate only as an asymptotic for the exponent $-\ln\rho$, and only from $u\approx12$ if a ten-percent relative error there is acceptable. (ii) At value sizes of $12$--$20$ nats use $\rho(u)\times0.88$--$0.91$.

\paragraph{Experimental design.} Bin by the quantity that enters the model, $u(v)=\ln v/\ln B$, computed per value, not by a proxy such as the scale of $N$; and always run a size-matched random control. Without a control, the $9$--$12\%$ deficit would have been misread as evidence against the randomness of the sieve pool.

\section{Discussion}

The overarching moral is that constraints on arithmetic sequences come in compensating pairs. The intuition ``the values $x^2-N$ are restricted, so they must be less smooth than random integers'' is natural and wrong, and it is wrong for a reason that generalises: the restriction is imposed by a symmetry group, and the same group that forbids half the primes doubles the density at the others. Orbit--stabiliser makes the bookkeeping exact.

This suggests a heuristic principle for judging whether an arithmetic pool can be modelled as random: identify the automorphism group $G$ of the generating map; expect availability to be divided by $|G|$ and density multiplied by $|G|$; expect the one-prime statistics to be exactly the random ones. Theorem~\ref{thm:univ} makes the ``expect'' unconditional at the level of averages. What the principle does \emph{not} control is joint behaviour across several primes, and Corollary~\ref{cor:where} isolates that as the only place a genuine effect could hide.

A second theme is the gulf between an asymptotic formula and a usable one. The leading-term Dickman expression is standard and asymptotically correct---but only as a statement about $\ln\rho$. As a statement about $\rho$ it exceeds $1$ throughout $(1,2]$, overshoots by more than ninefold at $u=2$, and, counter to the natural expectation, gets \emph{worse} as $u$ grows, reaching a factor $600$ at $u=40$. Even logarithmically it does not reach ten-percent relative accuracy until $u\approx12$---beyond the reach of the computations for which it is most often quoted.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The theorems proved here concern one-prime statistics and are exact. The empirical results concern global smoothness densities at $N\le2^{44}$, are statistical, and establish only that the sieve pool is indistinguishable from the random control at the achieved precision ($\pm2\%$); they cannot rule out smaller cross-prime correlations. Theorem~\ref{thm:window} uses only the crude first-order form of the finite-size correction. Finally, nothing here addresses the sieve's \emph{algorithmic} advantage---that smooth values are located by sieving rather than by testing---which is where the remaining practical gap between model and algorithm lies.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. Prime-power rigidity of the cancellation.} The cancellation $\#\text{admissible}\times\text{hit rate}=\text{period}$ is an orbit--stabiliser identity, and orbit--stabiliser survives Hensel lifting: for odd $p$ the squaring map on $(\mathbb Z/p^k)^\times$ has the same kernel $\{\pm1\}$. The pool should therefore be random-equivalent at every prime \emph{power}, i.e.\ in expected $p$-adic valuation, not merely in expected divisibility.

\paragraph{2. Second-order Dickman ratio law.} The divergence $L(u)/\rho(u)\to\infty$ is itself structured: the data are consistent with
\[
\frac{L(u)}{\rho(u)}\approx C\sqrt{2\pi u}\,\exp\!\left(\frac{u(\ln\ln u-1)}{\ln u}\right)
\]
up to a slowly varying factor ($9.1$ vs $31.0$ at $u=14.75$; $434$ vs $602$ at $u=40$). A high-precision anchor-verified $\rho$ table and the exact closed form on $(1,2]$ provide both a base case and reliable data to fit against.

\paragraph{3. Cross-prime independence as the only remaining degree of freedom.} Since one-prime statistics are pinned by the universality identity, any genuine deviation of $x^2-N$ from a random pool must be a correlation between distinct primes at one fixed $N$. Universality converts a vague question into a sharp, falsifiable one: measure joint divisibility statistics of pairs and triples of admissible primes against the independent-model prediction.

\paragraph{4. Beyond the quadratic sieve.} The number field sieve uses norms of algebraic integers in place of $x^2-N$; the relevant symmetry is the Galois group of the number field, and the analogue of Theorem~\ref{thm:os} would be a class-field-theoretic orbit--stabiliser statement.

\paragraph{5. Sharpening the sparsity bound.} Theorem~\ref{thm:sparse} is crude---it ignores the multiplicative constraint $\prod p^{e_p}\le x$ and is far from the true $\Psi(x,B)\approx x\rho(u)$. An unconditional bound of the form $x^{1-\epsilon(u)}$ by the same elementary injection, with $\epsilon$ explicit, would give a heuristic-free skeleton for the subexponential trade-off.

\section{Conclusion}

The quadratic-sieve relation pool is random-equivalent at one-prime resolution, exactly and at every scale, because the factor two lost to the quadratic-character constraint and the factor two gained in hit density are the same two: the order of the kernel of squaring in $(\Zp)^\times$. The phenomenon is universal---averaged over targets, no sieve map beats random---so the only remaining place a deviation could live is cross-prime correlation at fixed $N$. Large-scale measurement confirms the picture to within $2\%$ at every scale from $2^{32}$ to $2^{44}$. The residual $9$--$12\%$ shortfall against the Dickman prediction is carried identically by a random control and is the model's own finite-size correction, of magnitude $\ln\ln v/\ln v\in[0.1,0.25]$ on the experimental window, decaying to zero only logarithmically. The leading-term Dickman formula is not a probability below $u=3$, overshoots $\rho$ by a factor that grows without bound, and reaches ten-percent accuracy in the exponent only from $u\approx12$. Finally, the constraint that provoked the enquiry pays a dividend: relations live in half the factor base, so half as many are needed.

